media lives… backend a directory on disk <code>offsite_backup.media.filesystem.DirectorySource</code> <code>ROOT</code>, default <code>settings.MEDIA_ROOT</code> a Docker volume mounted into the container that runs the backup <code>offsite_backup.media.filesystem.DirectorySource</code> inside the container a volume is a directory a Docker volume not mounted there <code>offsite_backup.media.docker_volume.VolumeSource</code> <code>VOLUME</code>; the <code>docker</code> CLI and the daemon's socket an S3 bucket (any S3-compatible server) <code>offsite_backup.media.s3.S3Source</code> <code>[s3]</code> extra; <code>BUCKET</code>, <code>PREFIX</code> any Django storage (django-storages' S3, GCS, Azure, …) <code>offsite_backup.media.django_storage.StorageSource</code> <code>STORAGE</code> (an alias of <code>STORAGES</code>), <code>PREFIX</code> nowhere leave <code>MEDIA</code> out DirectoryS3 bucketDocker volume, not mountedDjango storage <pre><code>'MEDIA': [
    {
        'BACKEND': 'offsite_backup.media.filesystem.DirectorySource',
        'ROOT': '/app/media',
        'EXCLUDE': ['cache/*', '*/thumbs/*'],
    },
],
</code></pre> <p>Regular files under <code>ROOT</code>, in sorted order; names inside the tar are relative to <code>ROOT</code>. Symbolic links are not followed. A file that cannot be read is logged (<code>Media file not readable; left out</code>) and left out rather than failing the night — unless <code>STRICT</code> is on, and then it fails the pass, naming the file. A <code>ROOT</code> that does not exist is <code>W005</code> under <code>manage.py check --deploy</code> — a missing mount is worth knowing at start, not at 03:00 — and the pass logs <code>Media directory not found</code>. A plain <code>manage.py check</code> does not warn: the web service and a <code>migrate</code> container need no media mount (why).</p> <pre><code>'MEDIA': [
    {
        'BACKEND': 'offsite_backup.media.s3.S3Source',
        'ENDPOINT_URL': os.environ.get('MEDIA_S3_ENDPOINT', ''),
        'BUCKET': os.environ.get('MEDIA_S3_BUCKET', ''),
        'PREFIX': 'private/media/',
        'ACCESS_KEY': os.environ.get('MEDIA_S3_ACCESS_KEY', ''),
        'SECRET_KEY': os.environ.get('MEDIA_S3_SECRET_KEY', ''),
    },
],
</code></pre> <p>Every object under <code>PREFIX</code>, in key order, streamed straight from the bucket into the tar — nothing is staged on disk but the sealed volume. The object's full key is its name inside the archive. The source has its own credentials: a read-only key for the media bucket, not the backup store's. <code>ADDRESSING_STYLE</code>, <code>SIGNATURE_VERSION</code> and <code>VERIFY</code> work as on the store — a MinIO or SeaweedFS media bucket needs <code>'ADDRESSING_STYLE': 'path'</code> (S3 providers).</p> <pre><code>'MEDIA': [
    {
        'BACKEND': 'offsite_backup.media.docker_volume.VolumeSource',
        'VOLUME': 'acme_media',          # as `docker volume ls` shows it
    },
],
</code></pre> <p>For a worker that does not mount the volume. A throwaway container is created, never started — read-only mount, no network, label <code>offsite_backup=media</code> — and <code>docker cp &lt;container&gt;:/volume/. -</code> streams the volume through the daemon as one tar. The source cuts that one stream into volumes as it goes, so the Docker volume is read once and nothing but the sealed volume being written touches the disk; the container is removed afterwards, whatever happened. Nothing in the image runs, so any small image will do (<code>IMAGE</code>, default <code>busybox:stable</code>, pulled by <code>docker create</code> if absent).</p> <p>Regular files only: symbolic links are not followed, and a hard link — which one stream read once cannot carry — is logged (<code>Media file is a hard link; left out</code>) and left out, or fails the pass under <code>STRICT</code>. The volume is asked about first: <code>docker create</code> would make a missing named volume, empty, and a typo would then back up nothing every night. A missing volume is logged (<code>Media volume not found</code>), fails a <code>REQUIRED</code> source, and is <code>W005</code> (<code>E009</code> when required) under <code>check --deploy</code>; a daemon that cannot be asked is <code>E012</code>.</p> <p>The worker needs the <code>docker</code> CLI and the daemon's socket (<code>DOCKER_HOST</code> is honoured). Access to that socket is root on the host (Security model): mount the volume read-only into the worker and use <code>DirectorySource</code> wherever you can.</p> <pre><code>'MEDIA': [
    {
        'BACKEND': 'offsite_backup.media.django_storage.StorageSource',
        'STORAGE': 'default',            # an alias of settings.STORAGES
        'PREFIX': 'uploads/',
    },
],
</code></pre> <p>Whatever backend the project stores files with, through Django's own API: <code>listdir</code> from <code>PREFIX</code> down (files before folders at each level, sorted), <code>size</code>, and <code>open</code>, read a block at a time — no file is read whole. Names in the archive are the storage's names. A file deleted between listing and reading is logged and left out.</p> <p>django-storages' S3 file is read from its object's body directly (<code>file.obj.get()['Body']</code>, streamed): its own <code>read()</code> first downloads the whole object into a <code>SpooledTemporaryFile</code>, which stays in memory unless <code>AWS_S3_MAX_MEMORY_SIZE</code> is set. Other cloud backends spool a file the same way before handing it over — set their own max-memory setting (<code>GS_MAX_MEMORY_SIZE</code> for GCS) so large files spool to disk. For an S3 bucket, <code>S3Source</code> is the faster choice anyway: it lists in one pass where a storage asks for each file's size. A <code>STORAGE</code> that <code>STORAGES</code> does not define is <code>E001</code>; a <code>PREFIX</code> that cannot be listed under <code>check --deploy</code> is <code>E012</code>.</p>
